  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nRest of Today: \n\nDespite the ominous upstream radar earlier today, drier air at\nthe lower levels has substantially eroded the rainfall footprint\nthis afternoon (as expected). Thus expect occasional sprinkles\nand perhaps light showers sufficient to wet the ground in a few\nspots, favoring near and north of I-80 until sunset. Mostly\ncloudy skies will keep temperatures in the mid 70s to around\n80F, coolest northwest.\n\nTonight through Sunday Night:\n\nFollowing a mostly quiet evening, isolated to scattered showers\nwill likely blossom overnight (favoring near/north of the IL and\nKankakee Rivers) into Sunday morning. Forecast soundings are \nunfavorable for anything more than isolated embedded \nthunderstorms (slight chance/20% mention). Lows will be in the \nmid 60s to around 70F. \n\nSunday will mark the start of the advertised very warm to hot\nand humid pattern in store for much of the week. However,\nrelatively extensive cloud cover and a sharp lower level\ninversion will keep temperatures in check somewhat (mid to \nlocally upper 80s). Breezy southwesterly flow below the\ninversion will yield gusts up to 30 mph. A mid-level short-wave\nwill cross the mid-upper MS Valley in the afternoon, which will\nbring another chance (20-30%) for elevated convection (widely\nscattered showers and a few storms) to areas primarily north of\nI-80. Fairly meager, skinny CAPE due to marginal mid-level \nlapse rates rooted above the strong inversion down around 850 \nmb, and overall modest forcing will likely again serve to keep\nthunderstorms isolated at most.\n\nAdditional isolated to scattered showers (20-30% chance) and a \nfew storms may then move across portions of far northern \nIllinois late Sunday night/early Sunday. \n\nMonday Onward:\n\nStrong mid and upper level ridging will amplify northeastward \nfrom the southern Plains and center over the Ohio Valley next \nweek. While we're past peak crop evapotranspiration, it will be \na very warm to hot and generally uncomfortably humid week. \nMonday still looks to have a good chance to be the first 90F \nreading of August at Chicago and Rockford, on the last day of \nthe month! September and thus meteorological fall (already?!) \nwill get off to a summer-like start with additional 90+ temps \nand daytime heat indices flirting with 100F in spots probable on\nTuesday and Wednesday. If the ridge flattens out sufficiently \nlater in the week, weak surface high pressure may enable a \nbackdoor cold front to sag south and bring some cooling, \nparticularly closer to the lake. \n\nThe ridging currently looks amplified enough to result in more \npronounced capping and a better chance for \"ring of fire\" type \nconvective activity to remain primarily north of the area until \nperhaps later in the week, when the ridge may flatten out some, \nas alluded to above. Northern Illinois (especially with \nnorthward extent) is close enough to the precarious northern \nperiphery of the ridge to warrant occasional lower end (20-30%) \nthunderstorm chances (focused at night) through midweek. \nEnsemble guidance variance, even later in the week, is such that\nperiodic PoPs are still only in the 20-30% range. \n\nLooking ahead to Labor Day weekend, central CONUS ridging will \nlikely remain in control, resulting in continued well above \nnormal temps. The exact position and orientation of the ridging \nwill modulate how warm temperatures will get, along with \nconvective chances.\n\nCastro",
